@0    = ~[Husam]Ajantis, why do you dislike working with the Shadow Thieves so much? We worked together quite well already, I would say.~
@1    = ~[Ajantis]Do I really have to spell it out for you?! - Our past alliances were something completely different!~
@2    = ~[Husam]Indeed. This time, the Shadow Thieves are a viable option to reach where <CHARNAME> needs to go, and desparetly so - while the last time we worked together was nothing but convenience. Is that what you meant?~
@3    = ~[Husam]Indeed. This time, the Shadow Thieves offered a viable option to reach where <CHARNAME> needed to go, and desparetly so - while the last time we worked together was nothing but convenience. Is that what you meant?~
@4    = ~[Ajantis]That's not what I was referring to at all! Our past alliances were on... on a level of information exchange, of preventing diplomatic complications and keeping an eye on your evil organization's doings - not being *dependent* on them like here!~
@5    = ~[Husam]Ah, so joining forces with us evil Shadow Thieves in no problem for a paladin of Helm, as long as it isn't *crucial* for your goals. Did I understand it correctly now?~
@6    = ~[Ajantis]That's not...! You are twisting the words in my mouth!~
@7    = ~[Husam]Do I really? Well, as long as you can allign your actions with your god's dogmas, Ajantis... Let me assure you: I'm not overly happy to be depentent on a paladin from such a powerful order, either. But here I am - beaten and defenseless, and thankful for our alliance. You should consider this a success.~
@8    = ~So, Ajantis. You don't like travelling with me, because I am 'evil'.~
@9    = ~That you are! If I only imagine the things you did in the name of your guild I feel my heart ache. I tolerate your presence out of respect for <CHARNAME>'s decision, only.~
@10   = ~That is fine by me. Especially, since I feel the same about you.~
@11   = ~So, Ajantis - as a paladin, you do what is good and righteous?~
@12   = ~(sigh) I know where this is leading, it is always the same meager attempt of evil people by using an exaggerated, very special example to ensnarl me into contradicting myself.~
@13   = ~Well, it's not that hard to get there. Upholding the laws of an evil landlord isn't necessarily good, so there you have your contradiction.~
@14   = ~If it is an evil landlord, the laws are probably evil. An evil landlord has to be faught, for the sake of righteousness.~
@15   = ~I *know* what you are about to say. When do we know whether we have to obide the laws or fight the people making them? There is a higher order to things, Thief.~
@16   = ~A higher order? You are referring to the gods?~
@17   = ~Yes, Husam. I serve my Lord Helm, and I follow his dogmas, which define what is good and righteous.~
@18   = ~Which are?~
@19   = ~Vigilance for the weak, fulfilling your duty, and upholding the... (pauses) ...law. - Wipe that smile off your face, thief, or I will!~
@20   = ~Husam, you of course would prefer having an evil landlord, with evil laws that allow yourself to berich yourself.~
@21   = ~On the contrary, Ajantis. I, also, do enjoy the stability of a good-reigned land, with a law that gives stable possibilities for all inhabitants to prosper.~
@22   = ~Prosper? So that you have more you can steal!~
@23   = ~When you put it that way, it sounds unattractively short-sighted. I, also, think it's best if every citizen has a good set of laws they *want* to obey. It keeps the folks calm. I would advice every landlord to rule this way.~
@24   = ~I cannot believe you truly mean this. You and your organization of thieves profit from power struggles and general discontentment. Do not insult my senses!~
@25   = ~Oh, but I do mean it. I didn't say I would see myself oblidged to obey these laws *myself* now, of course.~
@26   = ~I knew it. Discussing with you is more than fruitless.~
